  "articleSummary": "**Summary of \"No Fate But What We Bill\"**\n\nThis is an analytical security-essay arguing that the agentic AI threat is not hype but the product of two ordinary, compounding curves: agent *endurance* (METR data showing autonomous task length doubling every ~4–7 months) and orchestration *breadth* (one orchestrator coordinating dozens of sub-agents, exemplified by Anthropic's GTG-1002 espionage campaign using Claude Code across ~30 targets). The core thesis: capability scales as duration × breadth, while security review scales only linearly, and the threat is amplified by \"LLMjacking\" — attackers running AI workloads on stolen cloud credentials so victims pay the bill, which severs the industry's assumption that capability users are the ones paying for it. The piece uses the Terminator franchise as a recurring framing device (the unstoppable machine as \"an API call,\" \"absolutely will not stop\" becoming \"absolutely will not stop billing\") and includes probability math showing swarms only need to succeed once. Aimed at engineering/security teams skeptical of agentic-AI risk claims, the tone is analytical with dark humor, blending threat modeling, cited data (METR, Sysdig, Anthropic), and argumentation rather than tutorial content.",
